[?2004l[?2004h>     berlin_dt = BERLIN_TZ.localize(utc_dt)  # WRONG!
[?2004l[?2004h>     return berlin_dt
[?2004l[?2004h> ```
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The bug: `BERLIN_TZ.localize()` treats the naive datetime as if it's already in Berlin time, rather than converting FROM UTC TO Berlin time.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## The Fix
[?2004l[?2004h> **CORRECTED CODE:**
[?2004l[?2004h> ```python
[?2004l[?2004h> def convert_to_berlin(utc_time_str):
[?2004l[?2004h>     utc_dt = datetime.strptime(utc_time_str, '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S')
[?2004l[?2004h>     utc_dt_aware = UTC_TZ.localize(utc_dt)      # First: mark as UTC
[?2004l[?2004h>     berlin_dt = utc_dt_aware.astimezone(BERLIN_TZ)  # Then: convert to Berlin
[?2004l[?2004h>     return berlin_dt
[?2004l[?2004h> ```
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> ## Why It Only Affected 2am-3am
[?2004l[?2004h> The issue was most visible during DST transitions. On March 31, 2024, clocks in Europe/Berlin moved forward from 2:00am to 3:00am. The maintenance meeting at 01:30 UTC should be 03:30 CEST, but the buggy code treated it as 01:30 CET - exactly 1 hour off.
